Let display-popup open its modal float for a control client, without
blocking that client's own command queue on it.

The bail predates 34cd5da4: a popup was a client-side overlay, so one asked
for by a client with no screen crashed the server (tmux-og #346), and af3e4d2
turned that into a silent no-op. A popup is now an ordinary modal floating
pane in the target window — a server-side object — and every client-dependent
input below already answers safely for a control client: status_line_size()
and status_at_line() short-circuit on CLIENT_CONTROL, and tty_window_offset()
reads cached zeroes off a tty that was never started, which is exactly the
window-relative placement wanted here.

Keeping the bail means any display-popup whose target client resolves to a
control client silently does nothing. That is not a corner: a shell inside a
session whose only client is a control client (tmux-og's remote-bridge daemon)
has no other client for cmd_find_best_client() to pick, so every popup-based
tool in a bridged session opens nothing at all.

Dropping only that bail is not enough on its own: past it, the function sets
new_wp->wait_item and returns CMD_RETURN_WAIT, which parks the queuing item
until the popup pane exits. For an ordinary command client that is the whole
point of -E — it blocks the shell that asked for the popup. But when the
command reached here off a control client's own queue (a remote `set-hook
after-split-window "display-popup -E ..."` fired by something the client
itself sent, or a display-popup the client sent directly), that is the same
queue the control client depends on for every other command it sends — so
waiting there wedges the client, including the keystrokes the popup needs to
make progress. Only that item's queuing client (cmdq_get_client(), not the
possibly-different target client used for placement) determines whether to
wait: return CMD_RETURN_NORMAL without setting wait_item when it is a control
client, and wait as before otherwise.

Submitted upstream; drop this file once it lands.

--- a/cmd-display-menu.c
+++ b/cmd-display-menu.c
@@ -392,6 +392,7 @@ cmd_display_popup_exec(struct cmd *self, struct cmdq_item *item)
 	struct cmd_find_state	*target = cmdq_get_target(item);
 	struct session		*s = target->s;
 	struct client		*tc = cmdq_get_target_client(item);
+	struct client		*c;
 	struct winlink		*wl = target->wl;
 	struct window		*w = wl->window;
 	struct window_pane	*wp = target->wp, *new_wp = NULL;
@@ -404,6 +405,7 @@ cmd_display_popup_exec(struct cmd *self, struct cmdq_item *item)
 	const char		*border_style = args_get(args, 'S');
 	char			*cause = NULL, *title = NULL;
 	enum pane_lines		 lines = PANE_LINES_SINGLE;
+	enum cmd_retval		 retval;
 	u_int			 px, py, sx, sy, count = args_count(args);
 	struct args_value	*av;
 	long long		 ll;
@@ -413,8 +415,6 @@ cmd_display_popup_exec(struct cmd *self, struct cmdq_item *item)
 			server_kill_pane(w->modal);
 		return (CMD_RETURN_NORMAL);
 	}
-	if (tc->flags & CLIENT_CONTROL)
-		return (CMD_RETURN_NORMAL);
 	if (w->modal != NULL)
 		return (CMD_RETURN_NORMAL);

@@ -560,13 +560,27 @@ cmd_display_popup_exec(struct cmd *self, struct cmdq_item *item)
 		events_fire("pane-title-changed", ep);
 	}

-	new_wp->wait_item = item;
+	/*
+	 * A control client's queue serves every command it sends the
+	 * server, itself included, so waiting here on a popup it started
+	 * would block that same queue on the popup's lifetime. Return
+	 * normally and let the popup run detached for that client only;
+	 * an ordinary command client still waits, since that is how -E
+	 * blocks its caller.
+	 */
+	c = cmdq_get_client(item);
+	if (c != NULL && (c->flags & CLIENT_CONTROL))
+		retval = CMD_RETURN_NORMAL;
+	else {
+		new_wp->wait_item = item;
+		retval = CMD_RETURN_WAIT;
+	}
 	server_redraw_session(s);
 	if (sc.argv != NULL)
 		cmd_free_argv(sc.argc, sc.argv);
 	environ_free(sc.environ);
 	free(title);
-	return (CMD_RETURN_WAIT);
+	return (retval);

 out:
 	if (sc.argv != NULL)
